            <ArticleTitle>Repeat Gamma-Knife Radiosurgery for Refractory or Recurrent Trigeminal Neuralgia with Consideration About the Optimal Second Dose.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>371-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.wneu.2015.08.056</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1878-8750(15)01072-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE">To investigate adequate radiation doses for repeat Gamma Knife radiosurgery (GKS) for trigeminal neuralgia in our series and meta-analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS">Fourteen patients treated by ipsilateral repeat GKS for trigeminal neuralgia were included. Median age of patients was 65 years (range, 28-78), the median target dose, 140-180). Patients were followed a median of 10.8 months (range, 1-151) after the second gamma-knife surgery. Brainstem dose analysis and vote-counting meta-analysis of 19 studies were performed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS">After the second gamma-knife radiosurgeries, pain was relieved effectively in 12 patients (86%; Barrow Neurological Institute Pain Intensity Score I-III). Post-gamma-knife radiosurgery trigeminal nerve deficits were mild in 5 patients. No serious anesthesia dolorosa was occurred. The second GKS radiation dose ≤ 60 Gy was significantly associated with worse pain control outcome (P = 0.018 in our series, permutation analysis of variance, and P = 0.009 in the meta-analysis, 2-tailed Fisher's exact test). Cumulative dose ≤ 140-150 Gy was significantly associated with poor pain control outcome (P = 0.033 in our series and P = 0.013 in the meta-analysis, 2-tailed Fisher's exact test). A cumulative brainstem edge dose &gt;12 Gy tended to be associated with trigeminal nerve deficit (P = 0.077).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION">Our study suggests that the second GKS dose is a potentially important factor.</AbstractText>
